  "schema_version": 1,
  "id": "a5e66aa3-04ae-5c23-bbed-2d2e47691363",
  "slug": "sticky-toffee-pudding-thumbprints-recipe-8781774",
  "name": "Sticky Toffee Pudding Thumbprints",
  "recipe_type": "dessert",
  "brief_description": "These sticky toffee pudding thumbprints are the world famous British dessert, made as a cookie! They have a chewy, date-filled cookie base and a rich, buttery toffee filling.",
  "cuisine": "American",
  "instructions": [
    {
      "position": 1,
      "text": "Place dates in a small heatproof bowl. Pour in enough boiling water to cover, then stir in baking soda, and soak dates for 10 minutes."
    },
    {
      "position": 2,
      "text": "Meanwhile, place 1 cup butter in a light colored saucepan over medium-low heat. Cook, stirring constantly, until butter is browned, 5 to 10 minutes. Pour browned butter into a large mixing bowl and allow to cool for 10 minutes."
    },
    {
      "position": 3,
      "text": "Drain dates; add to a food processor or blender, and pulse until mixture is thick and paste-like, with few large chunks of date remaining."
    },
    {
      "position": 4,
      "text": "To the browned butter, add brown sugar, molasses, vanilla, espresso powder, salt, nutmeg, cloves, and allspice; stir to combine. Add in egg and stir until well incorporated, about 1 minute. Add in dates, and stir until thoroughly combined. Add in 1 1/2 cups flour, cornstarch, and baking powder and mix until just combined. Add in remaining 1 cup flour and stir until just incorporated."
    },
    {
      "position": 5,
      "text": "Pour 1/2 cup white sugar in a small bowl."
    },
    {
      "position": 6,
      "text": "Scoop out 1 tablespoon dough and roll into a ball; roll in sugar until completely coated. Dough will still be so somewhat soft, but should still roll into a ball. Using your thumb, or the handle of a wooden spoon, spatula, or small whisk, make an indentation in the center, about 1/2-inch wide. Place cookies on a plate. Repeat this process with remaining dough. Refrigerate until chilled, at least 30 minutes."
    },
    {
      "position": 7,
      "text": "Preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (180 degrees C). Line baking sheets with silicone baking mats or parchment paper. Arrange chilled cookies 1 inch apart on prepared baking sheets."
    },
    {
      "position": 8,
      "text": "Bake until cookies begin to puff, and small cracks begin to appear, 12 to 14 minutes. Remove cookies from oven, and immediately use the handle of a wooden spoon or spatula to press the center of cookies back down. Cool cookies on the baking sheet for 5 minutes, then remove to a wire rack to cool completely."
    },
    {
      "position": 9,
      "text": "For toffee sauce, place brown sugar, butter, water, molasses, and salt in a heavy-bottomed sauce pot over medium-low heat. Cook, stirring constantly, until mixture is boiling. Reduce heat to low and boil for 2 minutes, stirring constantly. Carefully pour in cream (mixture will sputter), while continuing to stir. Return to a boil, and cook for 5 minutes, stirring constantly. Remove from heat; stir in vanilla and lemon juice. Pour into a heatproof glass measuring cup with a pour spout. Allow toffee sauce to cool until slightly thickened, stirring occasionally, 15 to 20 minutes."
    },
    {
      "position": 10,
      "text": "Carefully pour toffee sauce into center of each cookie, taking care to not overfill. Alternatively, you can spoon the sauce into the cookies instead of pouring from a measuring cup. Toffee sauce will thicken as it cools. Store cookies in a single layer in an airtight container."
    }
  ],
